+## Resolution-first decision
+
+The global obstruction is still coverage: finite receiver exclusions, K16, and the standard-F2 degree-125 interface do not land every hypothetical minimal counterexample. Within the live D125 work, however, there is a sharply bounded next proof step. I find a new conditional mechanism, `GOLD-36-SPLICE`: compare the nonzero order-36 bracket forced in the golden ramified Morse chart with the independently fixed unequal-face Jacobian scalar. The two scalar laws conflict. This would eliminate the entire **non-H-divisible** first-correction branch if the provisional two-regime source implication and this consumer pass review. It says nothing about the H-divisible branch, common polygons, all-degree coverage, or JC2.
+
+Global rank changes are deliberately sparse. I lower finite degree farming, serial-germ representations, plane descent from higher-dimensional counterexamples, and unguided sparse solving; raise the all-degree block/sandwich target and the productive Hamiltonian-centralizer/normal-form fragment. The immediate task priority is different: raise the golden order-36 scalar check above every new solver or abstraction, then attack the H-divisible correction with confluent branch jets. No AWS, solver, source expansion, or new machinery is warranted by this card.
+
